		{
			"id": "111294516399155622",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@stubbornella/111294516369496165",
			"author": {
				"name": "Nicole Sullivan",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@stubbornella",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/304/261/194/270/593/original/43ef87eef146046d.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-10-25T07:39:09.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> there are 3 ways to interperet massive developer dissatisfaction with web components</p><p>1. Survey issues<br>2. WC DX should be fixed<br>3. Sunk cost fallacy</p><p>For this survey we asked for awareness (never heard of it, heard of it, tried it) &amp; sentiment (positive and negative). We can (later) filter out folks who haven’t tried them to see if it is different</p><p>I want to know which of the interpretations makes sense, but not from the lovers or hater. It is average developers who matter</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
